  "provider_query": "Which organization does the CVE program identify as the source of CVE records used by NVD",
  "answer": "The CVE Program identifies the **CVE List** (operated by **The MITRE Corporation**) as the source of CVE records that feed the NVD.\n\n- CVE.org states that CVE and NVD are separate programs: the CVE List, launched by MITRE in 1999, feeds NVD, which enriches those records.  \n  https://www.cve.org/about/relatedefforts\n\n- The same relationship is repeated in the CVE Program FAQs: the CVE List feeds NVD so updates appear in NVD.  \n  https://www.cve.org/ResourcesSupport/FAQs\n\n- NVD states that the CVE Program is maintained by MITRE (sponsored by DHS/CISA) and that NVD is synchronized with the CVE List.  \n  https://nvd.nist.gov/general/FAQ-Sections/General-FAQs\n\n- NVD ingests the CVE List about hourly and enriches published records; it does not publish the original CVE records.  \n  https://nvd.nist.gov/general/FAQ-Sections/CVE-FAQs\n\n- NVD’s process page: the CVE program is maintained by MITRE; CNAs and MITRE assign IDs; published records typically appear in NVD within about an hour.  \n  https://nvd.nist.gov/general/cve-process\n\n- NVD developers’ guidance: the CVE Program is maintained by MITRE; NVD analyzes each CVE after it is published to the CVE List.  \n  https://nvd.nist.gov/developers\n\n- CVE records themselves are published by CVE Numbering Authorities (CNAs) and other program partners; MITRE also acts as Secretariat, Top-Level Root, and CNA of Last Resort.  \n  https://www.cve.org/About/Process\n\n- NVD later also ingests extra data from Authorized Data Publishers (notably CISA ADP), but the base CVE records still come from the CVE List.  \n  https://www.cve.org/ProgramOrganization/ADPs",
